# Licenses

This repository holds two distinct kinds of material, under two different licenses.

---

## 1. Wiki content — Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0)

All wiki content in this repository and published at https://disabilitywiki.org — the markdown
pages, articles, guides, and resource listings, in English and Spanish — is licensed under
CC BY-SA 4.0.

You are free to:

- **Share** — copy and redistribute the material in any medium or format
- **Adapt** — remix, transform, and build upon the material for any purpose, including commercially

Under the following terms:

- **Attribution** — You must give appropriate credit, provide a link to the license, and indicate
  if changes were made.
- **ShareAlike** — If you remix, transform, or build upon the material, you must distribute your
  contributions under this same license.
- **No additional restrictions** — You may not apply legal terms or technological measures that
  legally restrict others from doing anything the license permits.

The complete legal code is in [LICENSE-CONTENT](LICENSE-CONTENT), and at
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode
Plain-language summary: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

### What this means for contributors

Contributors retain copyright in what they write. By contributing, you agree to license your
contribution under CC BY-SA 4.0.

Because CC BY-SA is irrevocable and requires derivative works to carry the same license, content
contributed here **cannot later be made proprietary, paywalled, or closed** — including by the
repository owner or any future maintainer. That protection is the point of choosing this license.

### What ShareAlike does not restrict

**Facts are not copyrightable, and this license does not reach them.** Phone numbers, hours of
operation, income limits, filing deadlines, eligibility figures — anyone may take those and use
them however they like, in any product, under any terms. ShareAlike binds someone who copies the
*writing*, not someone who needs the *information*. If you run a clinic, an agency, or a helpline
and you just need the numbers, take them; nothing here applies to you.

### A request the license cannot make

Date your copy, and re-check crisis information before each new print run. Hotline numbers get
disconnected and services cut their hours without notice. A copy that looks current but is two
years stale can send someone to a dead number at the worst possible moment, which is worse than
no copy at all.

